\title{}
\date{2026-01-21T22:15:51Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Let #{r} = "It is raining today", #{e} = "I had
    eggs for breakfast", and #{c} = "I had cereal for
    breakfast."  Consider the sentence "Either it is raining, or I
  ate cereal and not eggs for breakfast."  We can translate this
  sentence into propositional logic as follows:
  ##{r \lor (c \land \neg e)}
  Note that it's not necessarily clear which kind of or to use here; it doesn't matter too
  much.  Also, the parenthesis may technically not be required, but it's probably
  best to include them.}
